# Zenodo reads this file when archiving a GitHub release, and GitHub renders it
# as "Cite this repository".
#
# ⛔ DO NOT ADD A `.zenodo.json` BESIDE IT WITHOUT DELETING THIS FILE'S ROLE FIRST.
# Zenodo's own guide (help.zenodo.org/docs/github/describe-software/zenodo-json/,
# read 2026-09-13): "If your repository contains both a .zenodo.json and a
# CITATION.cff file, Zenodo will only use the .zenodo.json metadata. The
# CITATION.cff will be completely ignored for the GitHub release archiving."
# ⇒ Two metadata registers do not merge — the second one SILENTLY disables the
# first, and the failure is invisible until the DOI is minted and cannot be
# withdrawn. The same guide: "If you don't need these Zenodo-specific features, a
# CITATION.cff file alone is sufficient." We do not need them yet. If a future
# head needs `grants`, `communities` or `related_identifiers` (e.g. to link the
# arXiv paper), they must MOVE this metadata into `.zenodo.json`, not add one.
cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
type: software
title: "x86lean: a differentially validated executable semantics of x86-64 user-level instructions in Lean 4"
abstract: >-
  A user-level x86-64 ISA semantics in Lean 4 — definitional, executable and
  kernel-checked — built from public sources only and differentially validated
  against public executable models. Developed as part of the SaltBench campaign.
authors:
  - family-names: Hickey
    given-names: Jason
    affiliation: Independent
repository-code: "https://github.com/jyh/x86lean"
license: Apache-2.0
keywords:
  - x86-64
  - instruction set semantics
  - Lean 4
  - interactive theorem proving
  - differential testing
  - formal verification
